    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study tests whether SAE latents are atomic, not whether individual neurons show the behavior in the claim versions. SAE latents are a related but different construct.",
    "gate_note": "The paper's own results concern BatchTopK SAE latents and their co-occurrence, fuzzing scores, and semantic specialization. Its references to neuron polysemanticity and superposition are background, not results about individual neurons. Reconstruction and language-model CE costs are not rows because they do not measure the claim's subject.",
    "test_reason": "The study's results concern BatchTopK SAE latents trained on residual-stream activations (GPT-2 large layer 20, Gemma 2 9B layer 23). It measures latent co-occurrence (mean |φ|), fuzzing scores and qualitative sense splits. It reports no measurement of individual model neurons (MLP or vision) responding to unrelated inputs. Superposition and neuron polysemanticity appear only as background. SAE-latent blending is a related but different construct, so the study tests no version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that joint training with a meta-SAE decomposability penalty yields more atomic SAE latents. On GPT-2 large: −7.5% mean |φ|, +7.6% fuzzing, and +0.6 pp CE overhead over the solo SAE. Gemma 2 9B results are directional (+8.6% ΔFuzz). Qualitative case studies show blended latents split into sense-specific ones.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study jointly trains a primary and meta SAE with a decomposability penalty; it does not reproduce the neuron-response measurements in the claim's original sources.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Outcomes vary across penalty settings: on GPT-2 large, λ2 = 0.3, σ2 = 1.0 is the selected configuration, while λ2 = 1.0 increases mean |φ|; Gemma co-occurrence results are mixed and directional. The study evaluates only one layer per model.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "On GPT-2 large, a meta-SAE decomposability penalty lowers mean |φ| among SAE latents by up to 7.5% (λ2=0.3, σ2=1.0). At λ2=1.0 it raises |φ|. Fuzzing scores rise 7.6% (0.230 to 0.248). Gemma 2 9B shows +8.6% ΔFuzz, but its |φ| results are mixed. Qualitative splits of blended latents are shown for 'too', 'let', 'cookies' and 'flash'.",
        "proposed_claim": "SAE latents can blend distinct semantic subspaces, and a training-time decomposability penalty can make them more atomic at a modest reconstruction cost. This says nothing directly about model neurons.",
